      "text": "Since the projection formula states that $proj_{\\textbf{a}} \\textbf{v} = \\frac{\\textbf{v} \\cdot \\textbf{a}}{\\|\\textbf{a}\\|^2} \\textbf{a}$, we need to apply this formula in two steps: find the dot product between $\\textbf{a}$ and $\\textbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ z \\end{pmatrix}$, and then normalize the dot product using $\\|\\textbf{a}\\|.$\n\\[\\frac{\\|( \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ z \\end{pmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}) \\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}}{\\|\\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\\|^2}.\\]The dot product between $\\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ z \\end{pmatrix}$ and $\\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}$ equals\n\n\\[\\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ z \\end{pmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix} = (\\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ z \\end{pmatrix}).\\]The former can be further simplified to $z + 15,$ and the latter can be further simplified to $3z + 15.$ Resulting formula is then\n\n\\[\\frac{z + 15}{(3z + 15)^2} \\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}.\\]Since the resulting vector is a scalar times $\\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix},$ we conclude that $z + 15 = 0.$ This tells us that $z = \\boxed{-15}.$ The answer is -15",
